window.PG_TRAILS = {"trails":[{"id":"women","url":"trails/women.html","title":"Women of Cape Ann","kicker":"by car, with a walk on Rocky Neck","span":"1850s to 1980","short":"A full day by car","lead":"Ten stops for the women who painted Gloucester, from Fitz Henry Lane’s circle in the 1850s to Nell Blaine’s harbor in the 1950s. Most stops are outdoors and free; the museum stop is the one place to see several of their paintings at once.","legs":[[0,3,"drive"],[3,7,"walk"],[7,10,"drive"]],"hero":{"id":"mellen-gloucester-harbor","alt":"Mary Blood Mellen, Gloucester Harbor (Laying Nets), about 1870"},"stops":[{"n":1,"name":"Stage Fort Park","who":"Mary Blood Mellen (1819–1886)","addr":"Hough Avenue, Gloucester","ll":[42.6057,-70.67845],"approx":false,"text":"A minister’s wife from Sterling, Massachusetts, Mellen came into Fitz Henry Lane’s world after her brother-in-law took the Universalist pulpit in Gloucester in 1855. By the middle of the decade she was working beside Lane, with free use of his drawings and at times his canvases…","works":[{"id":"mellen-field-beach","t":"Field Beach, Stage Fort Park","by":"Mary Blood Mellen","d":"c. 1850","img":false,"mus":"Cape Ann Museum, Gloucester"},{"id":"mellen-gloucester-harbor","t":"Gloucester Harbor (Laying Nets)","by":"Mary Blood Mellen","d":"about 1870","img":true,"mus":"Bowdoin College Museum of Art, Brunswick, Maine"}]},{"n":2,"name":"Cape Ann Museum","who":"The collection","addr":"27 Pleasant Street, Gloucester","ll":[42.61402,-70.66162],"approx":false,"text":"The museum owns Theresa Bernstein’s <i>Gloucester in Blue</i> (1925), Nell Blaine’s <i>Wharf Studio, Gloucester</i> (1958) and the archive of the Folly Cove Designers; the Rocky Neck Art Colony also credits it with Martha Walter’s <i>Rocky Neck Store and My Studio</i> (1920). Ask at the desk which are on view.","works":[{"id":"bernstein-gloucester-in-blue","t":"Gloucester in Blue","by":"Theresa Bernstein","d":"1925","img":false,"mus":"Cape Ann Museum, Gloucester"},{"id":"blaine-wharf-studio","t":"Wharf Studio, Gloucester","by":"Nell Blaine","d":"1958","img":false,"mus":"Cape Ann Museum, Gloucester"}]},{"n":3,"name":"Our Lady of Good Voyage","who":"Josephine Nivison (1883–1968)","addr":"142 Prospect Street, Gloucester","ll":[42.61718,-70.65715],"approx":false,"text":"In the summer of 1923 Jo Nivison painted <i>Church Towers, Gloucester</i>, a head-on view of this church’s twin towers. It was the summer she and Edward Hopper painted together here, and she encouraged him to take up watercolor. We have not found the painting in a public collection.","works":[]},{"n":4,"name":"Rocky Neck Guest House","who":"Theresa Bernstein (1890–2002)","addr":"1 Eastern Point Road, Gloucester","ll":[42.59873,-70.6548],"approx":false,"text":"Bernstein first came to Gloucester in 1916. She and the painter William Meyerowitz stayed here after their marriage in 1919, and in 1924 bought a house on Mount Pleasant Avenue in East Gloucester (a private home), where they hosted friends including Milton Avery. Both helped found the North Shore Arts Association.","works":[]},{"n":5,"name":"Martha Walter’s studio","who":"Martha Walter (1875–1976)","addr":"37 Rocky Neck Avenue, Gloucester","ll":[42.60581,-70.65739],"approx":false,"text":"A Philadelphia painter trained under William Merritt Chase, Walter had joined the Gloucester colony by 1913 and kept a studio and gallery on Rocky Neck from the later 1910s into the 1920s. The building is now a gallery.","works":[]},{"n":6,"name":"Sally Michel’s studio","who":"Sally Michel Avery (1902–2003)","addr":"77–78 Rocky Neck Avenue, Gloucester","ll":[42.60757,-70.6559],"approx":false,"text":"In 1924 Sally Michel rented the studio next to Milton Avery’s here. They married in 1926, and her work supported the family while he painted.","works":[]},{"n":7,"name":"Rocky Neck wharves","who":"Nell Blaine (1922–1996)","addr":"Rocky Neck Avenue, Gloucester","ll":[42.605,-70.657],"approx":true,"text":"Blaine rented studios on Rocky Neck in 1954 and 1958; the Whitney’s <i>Harbor and Green Cloth, II</i> dates from the second summer. From 1974 until her death she summered in East Gloucester. The marker is placed on the avenue; the studio addresses are not published.","works":[{"id":"blaine-harbor-green-cloth","t":"Harbor and Green Cloth, II","by":"Nell Blaine","d":"1958","img":false,"mus":"Whitney Museum of American Art, New York"}]},{"n":8,"name":"Bass Rocks","who":"Martha Walter","addr":"Atlantic Road, Gloucester","ll":[42.61426,-70.63532],"approx":false,"text":"Walter’s entries at the Pennsylvania Academy in 1915 included a crowded beach at Bass Rocks; Philadelphia critics praised the Gloucester picture. Parking along Atlantic Road is limited.","works":[]},{"n":9,"name":"Good Harbor Beach","who":"Theresa Bernstein","addr":"Thatcher Road, Gloucester","ll":[42.61954,-70.63143],"approx":false,"text":"Bernstein was still painting this beach around 1980, when she was about ninety: the Brooklyn Museum owns <i>Rabbits, Good Harbor Beach, Gloucester</i>. Summer parking is by fee.","works":[{"id":"bernstein-rabbits","t":"Rabbits, Good Harbor Beach, Gloucester","by":"Theresa Bernstein","d":"about 1980","img":false,"mus":"Brooklyn Museum, New York"}]},{"n":10,"name":"Folly Cove, Lanesville","who":"Virginia Lee Burton (1909–1968)","addr":"Washington Street (Route 127), Lanesville","ll":[42.68839,-70.64125],"approx":false,"text":"The children’s author of <i>Mike Mulligan and His Steam Shovel</i> taught block printing to her neighbors here and in 1941 founded the Folly Cove Designers, who printed textiles by hand until 1969. Their blocks, fabrics and records are at the Cape Ann Museum.","works":[]}]},{"id":"hopper","url":"trails/hopper.html","title":"Edward Hopper’s Gloucester","kicker":"a downtown walk, then a drive","span":"1912 to 1934","short":"About four hours, walk and drive","lead":"Hopper came to Gloucester in 1912 with his friend Leon Kroll, and again in the summers of 1923, 1924, 1926 and 1928, mostly painting houses in watercolor. Twelve of his subjects can still be found.","legs":[[0,8,"walk"],[8,12,"drive"]],"hero":null,"stops":[{"n":1,"name":"Cape Ann Museum","who":"Start","addr":"27 Pleasant Street","ll":[42.61402,-70.66162],"approx":false,"text":"Start here: the museum has the most Hopper context in town, and its docent walks cover the downtown houses.","works":[]},{"n":2,"name":"Universalist Church","who":"<i>Universalist Church</i>, 1926","addr":"10 Church Street","ll":[42.6133,-70.66623],"approx":false,"text":"The church on Church Street. The watercolor is at the Princeton University Art Museum.","works":[{"id":"hopper-universalist","t":"Universalist Church","by":"Edward Hopper","d":"1926","img":false,"mus":"Princeton University Art Museum"}]},{"n":3,"name":"Anderson’s House","who":"<i>Anderson’s House</i>","addr":"18 Western Avenue","ll":[42.61065,-70.66692],"approx":false,"text":"A tall yellow Italianate house just up Western Avenue from Stacy Boulevard. The watercolor is at the Museum of Fine Arts, Boston.","works":[{"id":"hopper-andersons","t":"Anderson's House","by":"Edward Hopper","d":"1926 per museum (inscribed “Gloucester 1923”)","img":false,"mus":"Museum of Fine Arts, Boston"}]},{"n":4,"name":"The Fort","who":"<i>Italian Quarter, Gloucester</i>, 1912","addr":"Fort Square, off Commercial Street","ll":[42.60832,-70.66457],"approx":false,"text":"On his first visit, in 1912, Hopper painted the fishermen’s houses of the Fort, Gloucester’s Italian neighborhood.","works":[{"id":"hopper-italian-quarter","t":"Italian Quarter, Gloucester","by":"Edward Hopper","d":"1912","img":false,"mus":"Whitney Museum of American Art, New York"}]},{"n":5,"name":"Haskell’s House","who":"<i>Haskell’s House</i>, 1924","addr":"316 Main Street, at Prospect Street","ll":[42.61595,-70.65585],"approx":false,"text":"The Second Empire house at the corner of Main and Prospect Streets. The watercolor is at the National Gallery of Art.","works":[{"id":"hopper-haskells-house","t":"Haskell's House","by":"Edward Hopper","d":"1924","img":false,"mus":"National Gallery of Art, Washington"}]},{"n":6,"name":"Our Lady of Good Voyage","who":"Hopper and Jo Nivison, 1923","addr":"from Mount Vernon Street","ll":[42.61775,-70.6575],"approx":false,"text":"Hopper painted the church’s towers from a distance, from about 15–17 Mount Vernon Street; in the same summer Jo Nivison painted them head-on.","works":[]},{"n":7,"name":"Sun on Prospect Street","who":"<i>Sun on Prospect Street</i>, 1934","addr":"98–102 Prospect Street","ll":[42.61593,-70.66034],"approx":false,"text":"An oil, at the Cincinnati Art Museum.","works":[{"id":"hopper-prospect","t":"Sun on Prospect Street (Gloucester, Massachusetts)","by":"Edward Hopper","d":"1934","img":false,"mus":"Cincinnati Art Museum"}]},{"n":8,"name":"Freight cars and the box factory","who":"<i>Freight Cars</i> and <i>Box Factory</i>, 1928","addr":"Maplewood Avenue and Cleveland Street","ll":[42.61943,-70.66763],"approx":false,"text":"The rail yard by the station gave Hopper <i>Freight Cars, Gloucester</i>, with the steeple of St. Ann’s Church behind the cars; the box factory on Cleveland Street has been torn down. The station is the end of the walk, with trains back to Boston.","works":[{"id":"hopper-freight-cars","t":"Freight Cars, Gloucester","by":"Edward Hopper","d":"1928","img":false,"mus":"Addison Gallery of American Art, Andover"},{"id":"hopper-box-factory","t":"Box Factory, Gloucester","by":"Edward Hopper","d":"1928","img":false,"mus":"The Museum of Modern Art, New York"}]},{"n":9,"name":"The Mansard Roof","who":"<i>The Mansard Roof</i>, 1923","addr":"2 Clarendon Street, Rocky Neck","ll":[42.60666,-70.65874],"approx":false,"text":"The Brooklyn Museum bought this watercolor after its 1923 show. The house was part of Augustus Wonson’s estate.","works":[{"id":"hopper-mansard","t":"The Mansard Roof","by":"Edward Hopper","d":"1923","img":false,"mus":"Brooklyn Museum"}]},{"n":10,"name":"Above Cripple Cove","who":"<i>Gloucester Harbor</i>, 1912","addr":"East Main Street, East Gloucester","ll":[42.61505,-70.6484],"approx":true,"text":"Hopper’s 1912 oil of the harbor was painted from the ground above Cripple Cove. The marker is the cove; the exact spot is not recorded.","works":[{"id":"hopper-gloucester-harbor","t":"Gloucester Harbor","by":"Edward Hopper","d":"1912","img":false,"mus":"Whitney Museum of American Art, New York"}]},{"n":11,"name":"Briar Neck","who":"<i>Briar Neck, Gloucester</i>, 1912","addr":"Good Harbor Beach","ll":[42.61954,-70.63143],"approx":true,"text":"The point at the end of Good Harbor Beach. The marker is the beach.","works":[{"id":"hopper-briar-neck","t":"Briar Neck, Gloucester","by":"Edward Hopper","d":"1912","img":false,"mus":"Whitney Museum of American Art, New York"}]},{"n":12,"name":"Squam Light","who":"<i>Houses of ’Squam Light</i>, 1923","addr":"Wigwam Point, Annisquam","ll":[42.6619,-70.68149],"approx":false,"text":"Hopper’s view of the light and its keeper’s houses. The light can be seen from Wingaersheek Beach across the river; the keeper’s house is Coast Guard housing (see site 8).","works":[{"id":"hopper-squam-light","t":"Houses of 'Squam Light, Gloucester","by":"Edward Hopper","d":"1923","img":false,"mus":"Museum of Fine Arts, Boston"}]}]},{"id":"rothko","url":"trails/rothko.html","title":"Rothko, Avery and Gottlieb","kicker":"East Gloucester","span":"1921 to 1945","short":"Half a day","lead":"In 1932 Milton Avery invited two young New York painters, Mark Rothko and Adolph Gottlieb, to Gloucester. They drew the bathers on the beaches and the wharfs across the harbor. Six stops, from the beach to the fish flakes.","legs":[[0,2,"drive"],[2,5,"walk"],[5,6,"drive"]],"hero":null,"stops":[{"n":1,"name":"Cape Ann Museum","who":"Rothko’s wharf","addr":"27 Pleasant Street","ll":[42.61402,-70.66162],"approx":false,"text":"In August 2026 Christopher Rothko gave the museum his father’s <i>Untitled (wharf, Gloucester, Massachusetts)</i> of 1934, the first Rothko in its collection. It shows the salt-fish sheds of the Reed and Gamage wharfs near Smith Cove, painted from Banner Hill.","works":[{"id":"rothko-wharf-cam","t":"Untitled (wharf, Gloucester, Massachusetts)","by":"Mark Rothko","d":"1934","img":false,"mus":"Cape Ann Museum, Gloucester"}]},{"n":2,"name":"Good Harbor Beach","who":"Bathers and handball","addr":"Thatcher Road","ll":[42.61954,-70.63143],"approx":false,"text":"Avery painted the crowd watching the total solar eclipse of 1932 at Good Harbor. Two years later the friends met here daily: Edith Rothko remembered that they would “go swimming and play handball,” and Rothko painted the game.","works":[]},{"n":3,"name":"Avery’s studio","who":"Milton Avery (1885–1965)","addr":"77–78 Rocky Neck Avenue","ll":[42.60757,-70.6559],"approx":false,"text":"Avery first summered in Gloucester in 1921; in 1926 he and the sculptor Leonard Craske rented cottages here, the one record of where he stayed. His <i>Gloucester Dawn</i> of about 1921 is at Vassar.","works":[{"id":"avery-dawn","t":"Gloucester Dawn","by":"Milton Avery","d":"c. 1921","img":false,"mus":"Frances Lehman Loeb Art Center, Vassar College"}]},{"n":4,"name":"Smith Cove","who":"The wharfs","addr":"Rocky Neck causeway","ll":[42.60665,-70.65528],"approx":true,"text":"Smith Cove was the center of Gloucester’s gill-net fishing. Rothko painted its wharfs again and again in 1934: the Reed and Gamage salt-fish sheds with a water tank on the roof, and rows of fish flakes covered with white cloths to keep the sun off the fillets; fishermen among the gill-net reels on a wharf…","works":[{"id":"rothko-wharf-nga","t":"Untitled (wharf, Gloucester, Massachusetts)","by":"Mark Rothko","d":"1934","img":false,"mus":"National Gallery of Art, Washington"}]},{"n":5,"name":"Banner Hill","who":"The high view","addr":"above 186–220 East Main Street","ll":[42.60644,-70.65084],"approx":true,"text":"The ridge above Smith Cove, where the Impressionists painted. Rothko painted the Reed and Gamage wharfs from Banner Hill, and Gottlieb sketched them from a similar spot, possibly beside him. The hilltop is private; look from the road.","works":[]},{"n":6,"name":"Calder Street","who":"Rothko, summer 1934","addr":"Calder Street, East Gloucester","ll":[42.60897,-70.64384],"approx":true,"text":"In the summer of 1934 Rothko and his wife, Edith Sachar, rented a house on Calder Street, about half a mile from the wharfs, while the Averys and the Gottliebs summered nearby. The Rothkos arrived first and took the house the Gottliebs had wanted. The house number is not recorded; this is a quiet residential street.","works":[]}]},{"id":"waterfront","url":"waterfront.html","title":"The working waterfront","kicker":"the harbor at work","span":"Fish flakes to draggers","short":"A harbor walk, then a short drive","lead":"The harbor in the paintings was a factory. This page explains what the painters were looking at — fish flakes, dories, schooners, nets — and where the working waterfront can still be seen.","legs":[[0,4,"walk"],[4,5,"drive"]],"hero":{"id":"homer-schooners-waterboat","alt":"Winslow Homer, Gloucester Schooners and Waterboat, 1880"},"stops":[{"n":1,"name":"Fishermen’s Memorial","who":"","addr":"Stacy Boulevard at Babson Court","ll":[42.610051,-70.670223],"approx":false,"text":"Leonard Craske’s <i>Man at the Wheel</i>, dedicated August 23, 1925. The plaques list 5,368 men known to have been lost at sea; a single storm in 1862 took 120 men, and storms in 1879 took 159.","works":[]},{"n":2,"name":"Salting Fish marker","who":"","addr":"25 Rogers Street, at Porter Street","ll":[42.61171,-70.66452],"approx":false,"text":"At its peak Gorton’s ran dozens of wharves and many flake yards, where cod from the North Atlantic banks was split, salted and dried.","works":[]},{"n":3,"name":"Maritime Gloucester","who":"","addr":"Harbor Loop","ll":[42.61166,-70.66069],"approx":false,"text":"The harbor’s museum of its own working history, on the inner harbor.","works":[]},{"n":4,"name":"The Fort","who":"","addr":"Fort Square","ll":[42.60832,-70.66457],"approx":false,"text":"The point where Lane painted Ten Pound Island and Hopper painted the fishermen’s houses of the Italian Quarter.","works":[]},{"n":5,"name":"Smith Cove","who":"","addr":"East Gloucester","ll":[42.60665,-70.65528],"approx":false,"text":"Rothko’s 1934 wharfs had fish flakes on the pier, the last generation of the racks Lane had painted eighty years before.","works":[]}]}],"words":[{"t":"Fish flakes","c":"The catch and the cure","d":"Low wooden racks on which split, salted cod was laid out in the sun to dry. Whole fields of them appear in Lane’s and later paintings of the waterfront.","w":null},{"t":"Gill nets","c":"Grounds, lines and nets","d":"Nets hung like a curtain in the water, catching fish by the gills. Mellen’s <i>Gloucester Harbor (Laying Nets)</i> shows crews setting them.","w":{"id":"mellen-gloucester-harbor","t":"Gloucester Harbor (Laying Nets)","by":"Mary Blood Mellen","d":"about 1870","img":true,"mus":"Bowdoin College Museum of Art, Brunswick, Maine"}},{"t":"Dories","c":"The dory and its gear","d":"Small flat-bottomed boats carried on the schooners and rowed out to set lines and nets; Gordon Parks photographed Gloucester crews laying nets by hand from them in 1943. Homer drew boys in dories in 1873.","w":{"id":"homer-boys-in-a-dory","t":"Boys in a Dory","by":"Winslow Homer","d":"1873","img":true,"mus":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York"}},{"t":"Schooners","c":"Vessels","d":"The fore-and-aft rigged vessels of the Gloucester fleet that brought fish home from the banks.","w":{"id":"homer-schooners-waterboat","t":"Gloucester Schooners and Waterboat","by":"Winslow Homer","d":"1880","img":true,"mus":"Philadelphia Museum of Art"}},{"t":"Draggers","c":"Vessels","d":"Motor trawlers that drag a net along the bottom; Emile Gruppé painted them at the wharves.","w":null},{"t":"Drydock","c":"Wharves and the harbor","d":"A berth where a vessel is taken out of the water for repair. Hassam painted one in the 1890s.","w":{"id":"hassam-drydock","t":"Drydock, Gloucester","by":"Childe Hassam","d":"c. 1890s","img":true,"mus":"The Phillips Collection, Washington"}},{"t":"Shallop","c":"Vessels","d":"A small colonial working boat, the ancestor of many New England fishing craft, among them the Chebacco boat, the dogbody and the pinky.","w":null},{"t":"Chebacco boat","c":"Vessels","d":"A heavily built two-masted fishing boat that grew out of the shallop in Chebacco Parish, the part of Ipswich that became the town of Essex in 1819. The name was in use in the second half of the 1700s.","w":null},{"t":"Dogbody","c":"Vessels","d":"The square-sterned version of the Chebacco boat. Where the name came from is now forgotten.","w":null},{"t":"Pinky","c":"Vessels","d":"A schooner-rigged fishing vessel that descended from the Chebacco boat at the end of the 1700s. The name refers to its narrow “pink” stern, from a Dutch word for pinched.","w":null},{"t":"Jigger","c":"Vessels","d":"A Chebacco boat converted to a schooner rig by adding a bowsprit and a jib. Gloucester’s fishing timeline records jiggers of 30 tons and more built for mackerel fishing in 1816.","w":null},{"t":"Sharpshooter","c":"Vessels","d":"The new fishing schooner of the 1840s: a long hull, a straight keel, sharp bilges and the first hint of a hollow, slicing bow. Lane’s <i>Ships in Ice off Ten Pound Island</i> shows fishing schooners with the finer hulls of the sharpshooters and clippers.","w":{"id":"lane-ships-in-ice","t":"Ships in Ice off Ten Pound Island, Gloucester","by":"Fitz Henry Lane","d":"1850s","img":true,"mus":"Museum of Fine Arts, Boston"}},{"t":"Clipper schooner","c":"Vessels","d":"A fast fishing schooner: sharp-ended, shallow, beamy and over-canvassed. The first clipper owned in Gloucester was built at Essex in 1850; the <i>Smuggler</i> of 1877 was one.","w":null},{"t":"Plumb-stem schooner","c":"Vessels","d":"Dennison J. Lawlor’s design, with an upright stem and a deeper, more stable hull than the clippers. The Fish Commission’s research schooner <i>Grampus</i>, launched in 1885, helped set the new pattern.","w":null},{"t":"Fredonia schooner","c":"Vessels","d":"Schooners named after their prototype, the <i>Fredonia</i>: a graceful bow, a roomier foredeck and the rounded stern typical of the Gloucesterman.","w":null},{"t":"Knockabout","c":"Vessels","d":"A schooner with no bowsprit. The first was built in Essex in 1902 to make the work safer for fishermen; Joseph Garland counts the type among the last of the classic Gloucester schooners.","w":null},{"t":"Gloucesterman","c":"Vessels","d":"The classic Gloucester fishing schooner of the late 1800s. Built for a hard-fought trade without engines, it was sailed hard, because speed on the voyage home meant success.","w":null},{"t":"Tern","c":"Vessels","d":"A schooner with three masts.","w":null},{"t":"Georgesman","c":"Vessels","d":"A schooner that fished Georges Bank. Gloucester was using the name by the 1850s.","w":null},{"t":"Salt banker","c":"Vessels","d":"A schooner that sailed from Gloucester to the Grand Banks of Newfoundland for cod. Kipling’s <i>Captains Courageous</i> (1897) gives an accurate picture of their life.","w":null},{"t":"Auxiliary","c":"Vessels","d":"A sailing vessel with an engine as well. Gloucester’s first fishing vessel with an auxiliary engine came in 1900, and in the 1920s the fleet began converting to engines, mostly diesel.","w":null},{"t":"Coasting schooner","c":"Vessels","d":"Any merchant schooner carrying cargo from one port on the coast to another.","w":null},{"t":"Packet","c":"Vessels","d":"A vessel that carried passengers and higher-value goods between particular ports on a regular schedule. The packet sloops were the most elegant.","w":null},{"t":"Stone sloop","c":"Vessels","d":"A heavily built, undecorated sloop that carried granite blocks from the quarry piers around Cape Ann to other ports.","w":null},{"t":"Half brig","c":"Vessels","d":"The New England name for the hermaphrodite brig: square sails on the foremast only, and a spanker and gaff topsail on the main. It was the commonest brig in the coasting trade.","w":null},{"t":"Salt ship","c":"Vessels","d":"A ship or bark that brought salt to Gloucester for curing fish. Too deep to reach the wharves fully loaded, it was partly unloaded at anchor first.","w":null},{"t":"Lighter","c":"Vessels","d":"A small vessel that takes cargo off a deep-loaded ship so that she can reach a wharf. Gloucester did much of this “lightering” in the Stream.","w":null},{"t":"Yawl boat","c":"Vessels","d":"A schooner’s own boat, usually twenty feet long or less, round-bottomed and square-sterned, hung in davits at the stern. It was the lifeboat at sea and carried the captain and crew ashore in port. Lane painted one frozen into the harbor ice.","w":{"id":"lane-ships-in-ice","t":"Ships in Ice off Ten Pound Island, Gloucester","by":"Fitz Henry Lane","d":"1850s","img":true,"mus":"Museum of Fine Arts, Boston"}},{"t":"Moses boat","c":"Vessels","d":"A boat carried in a schooner’s stern davits and used to bring barrels of rum and molasses out from a beach.","w":null},{"t":"Wherry","c":"Vessels","d":"A larger, wider relative of the dory, used for bulky coastal gear such as gill nets and fish traps.","w":null},{"t":"Seine boat","c":"Vessels","d":"The big rowing boat used in seining. During St. Peter’s Fiesta, crews still race seine boats a half mile out from Pavilion Beach and back.","w":null},{"t":"Bowsprit","c":"Rig and deck","d":"The long spar at the bow that carries the headsails out over the water. Sailors called it the widow-maker, because men were lost falling from it while tending sail.","w":null},{"t":"Gaff","c":"Rig and deck","d":"On a schooner, the spar along the head of a four-sided sail. In a dory, a gaff was the hook carried for bringing fish aboard.","w":null},{"t":"Foremast and mainmast","c":"Rig and deck","d":"A schooner has at least two masts: the foremast, nearest the bow, and the mainmast behind it, the taller of the two.","w":null},{"t":"Topmast","c":"Rig and deck","d":"A second spar carried on top of a lower mast, used to fly more sail.","w":null},{"t":"Jumbo","c":"Rig and deck","d":"The larger of a schooner’s headsails, set forward of the foremast with the jib.","w":null},{"t":"Yankee","c":"Rig and deck","d":"A headsail flown above and forward of the jib, usually on vessels with a bowsprit.","w":null},{"t":"Queen topsail","c":"Rig and deck","d":"A small staysail set between the foremast and the mainmast.","w":null},{"t":"Halyards","c":"Rig and deck","d":"The lines that hoist the sails, and the gaffs and booms that hold them.","w":null},{"t":"Lazyjacks","c":"Rig and deck","d":"Lines from the topping lifts to under the boom that catch the sail like a net when it is lowered.","w":null},{"t":"Baggywrinkle","c":"Rig and deck","d":"Clumps of frayed rope tied into the rigging so that the sails do not chafe against the lines.","w":null},{"t":"Hanging in the gear","c":"Rig and deck","d":"Sails partly lowered or clewed up to dry, as Lane drew them on a half brig in 1851.","w":null},{"t":"Crosstrees","c":"Rig and deck","d":"Horizontal timbers high on the mast that spread the shrouds.","w":null},{"t":"Davits","c":"Rig and deck","d":"Small cranes, usually at the stern, for raising and lowering a boat. Banks schooners carried their yawl boats in stern davits.","w":null},{"t":"Scuppers","c":"Rig and deck","d":"Holes in the vessel’s sides at deck level that drain water overboard.","w":null},{"t":"Fo’c’sle","c":"Rig and deck","d":"Short for forecastle: the crew’s quarters in the forward part of the hull.","w":null},{"t":"Charley Noble","c":"Rig and deck","d":"The stovepipe of the galley, the ship’s kitchen.","w":null},{"t":"Pilothouse","c":"Rig and deck","d":"A small cabin on deck that shelters the wheel and the helmsman.","w":null},{"t":"Iron Mike","c":"Rig and deck","d":"Fishermen’s name for the autopilot, the electrical device that holds a vessel on a set course.","w":null},{"t":"Gypsyhead","c":"Rig and deck","d":"A metal drum with a smooth, curved face, usually mounted on a winch.","w":null},{"t":"Slaughterhouse","c":"Rig and deck","d":"The square compartments of the fish hold along the centerline over the keel, between the pens on either side.","w":null},{"t":"Pen boards","c":"Rig and deck","d":"Removable dividers of wood or metal that slot into the hold to make pens; when all are in, the hold is “penned up”. Dorymen used smaller ones to section off the dory for fish.","w":null},{"t":"Doryman","c":"The dory and its gear","d":"One of the two men who fished from a dory.","w":null},{"t":"Dory trawling","c":"The dory and its gear","d":"Fishing long trawl lines of baited hooks from six to ten dories carried on a schooner. The Gloucester schooner <i>Anna</i> made a successful dory-trawling trip to the Grand Banks in 1854, but Gloucester was slow to take up the method. Gloucester’s timeline marks 1941 as the year draggers replaced dory fishing.","w":null},{"t":"Trawl","c":"The dory and its gear","d":"A long ground line, buoyed and anchored, hung with many short lines of baited hooks. In 1881 a tub of haddock trawl held 500 hooks on about 292 fathoms of line.","w":null},{"t":"Trawl tub","c":"The dory and its gear","d":"A half-barrel holding a coiled trawl and its hooks. In 1881 a dory set out with six to eight of them.","w":null},{"t":"Slivers","c":"The dory and its gear","d":"Strips of salted menhaden, the main bait for the winter haddock trawls of 1881; when menhaden ran short, fresh herring took its place. Baiting a tub of 500 hooks took 45 minutes to an hour.","w":null},{"t":"Dory anchor","c":"The dory and its gear","d":"A small iron anchor that holds one end of a trawl to the bottom.","w":null},{"t":"Buoy and black ball","c":"The dory and its gear","d":"The float marking a trawl: a numbered wooden barrel with a flag. In 1881 each end buoy also carried a black ball.","w":null},{"t":"Gurdy","c":"The dory and its gear","d":"A hand crank fitted across the bow of a dory to haul in lines heavy with fish.","w":null},{"t":"Nippers","c":"The dory and its gear","d":"Doughnut-shaped grips that protected a fisherman’s fingers from the running trawl line. In 1881 each dory carried two pairs, made of wool.","w":null},{"t":"Gob stick","c":"The dory and its gear","d":"A heavy two-pronged wooden stick for stunning fish and taking hooks from their throats.","w":null},{"t":"Halibut killer","c":"The dory and its gear","d":"A heavy wooden club for stunning or killing big fish such as halibut, which can weigh more than 200 pounds.","w":null},{"t":"Bailer","c":"The dory and its gear","d":"A wooden or metal scoop for throwing water out of the dory.","w":null},{"t":"Tholepins","c":"The dory and its gear","d":"Narrow wooden pegs set in a dory’s rail that hold the oars in place while rowing.","w":null},{"t":"Fog horn","c":"The dory and its gear","d":"Dorymen carried a fog horn and a kerosene lamp to signal the schooner, by sound or by light, in thick fog or darkness.","w":null},{"t":"Tombstone","c":"The dory and its gear","d":"The dory’s narrow, V-shaped stern board.","w":null},{"t":"Pay out","c":"The dory and its gear","d":"To feed a line over the side, hand over hand.","w":null},{"t":"Hand-lining","c":"Grounds, lines and nets","d":"Fishing with a single line carrying one or two hooks. Dory trawling, with many hooks on one line, later replaced it.","w":null},{"t":"Sling ding","c":"Grounds, lines and nets","d":"A metal rod that held the two hooks of a Georges Bank hand line 12 to 18 inches apart.","w":null},{"t":"Highliner","c":"Grounds, lines and nets","d":"The top boat, or the top fisherman, of the fleet. The word goes back to fishing from the deck, when the best fisherman held the highest place in the bow.","w":null},{"t":"Purse seine","c":"Grounds, lines and nets","d":"A net about 1,000 feet long set in a circle around a school of mackerel. It replaced hand-lining for mackerel; Gloucester’s timeline dates it to 1848.","w":null},{"t":"Otter trawl","c":"Grounds, lines and nets","d":"A net towed along the bottom and held open by two otter doors. Its arrival in 1905 changed fishing on the New England banks.","w":null},{"t":"Footrope and headrope","c":"Grounds, lines and nets","d":"The bottom and top lines of a trawl net. Floats of metal, plastic or glass on the headrope hold the mouth of the net open.","w":null},{"t":"Warps","c":"Grounds, lines and nets","d":"The cables from a trawler’s winches to the two sides of the net, which carry the pull of the boat to the trawl.","w":null},{"t":"Georges","c":"Grounds, lines and nets","d":"Fishermen’s short name for Georges Bank. The Fish Commission’s reports from Gloucester in 1883 list the cod and halibut landed “from George’s Bank” month by month.","w":null},{"t":"Grand Banks","c":"Grounds, lines and nets","d":"The great fishing banks off Newfoundland, where Gloucester’s salt bankers went for cod.","w":null},{"t":"Fare","c":"Grounds, lines and nets","d":"A vessel’s catch as landed. A report from Gloucester in December 1883 notes that the vessels from Georges Bank “all brought in good fares of cod”.","w":null},{"t":"Trip","c":"Grounds, lines and nets","d":"Both the voyage and the fish it brings home: the whole catch aboard is “the trip”.","w":null},{"t":"Home run","c":"Grounds, lines and nets","d":"The passage from the fishing grounds back to the home port.","w":null},{"t":"Shore fishery","c":"Grounds, lines and nets","d":"Fishing near home in small vessels and boats. In November 1883 Gloucester’s shore fisheries had 56 vessels and 448 men.","w":null},{"t":"Groundfish","c":"Grounds, lines and nets","d":"Fish that live near the bottom, such as cod and haddock, the mainstay of the New England fisheries.","w":null},{"t":"Ice","c":"Grounds, lines and nets","d":"Packing fish in ice instead of salt became common about 1845, so that fresh fish could reach market. With faster schooners and the railroad, it revived Gloucester’s fishing. The ice was cut from fresh-water ponds.","w":null},{"t":"Fast freezing","c":"Grounds, lines and nets","d":"Clarence Birdseye’s quick-freezing method, which in 1924 started the processing of frozen fish.","w":null},{"t":"Lobster trap","c":"Grounds, lines and nets","d":"A baited trap set on the bottom, one of the traps of the coastal fisheries in Lane’s time. Homer painted three boys in a dory with lobster pots in 1875.","w":{"id":"homer-three-boys-lobster-pots","t":"Three Boys in a Dory with Lobster Pots","by":"Winslow Homer","d":"1875","img":true,"mus":"Nelson-Atkins Museum of Art, Kansas City"}},{"t":"Flake yard","c":"The catch and the cure","d":"A field of fish flakes beside the wharves. Lane’s 1836 view of Gloucester shows the Wonson family’s flake yard in East Gloucester, and Five Pound Island later became one.","w":null},{"t":"Dressing gang","c":"The catch and the cure","d":"The crew that cleaned cod on deck: the throater cut down the belly and took off the head, the gutter removed the insides but saved the liver, and the splitter took out the backbone.","w":null},{"t":"Gurry","c":"The catch and the cure","d":"The slime, blood and fluids spilled when fish are dressed. Schooners kept a gurry pen on deck.","w":null},{"t":"Kench cure","c":"The catch and the cure","d":"A salt cure that used more salt than dunfish but less than pickled “green” cod.","w":null},{"t":"Dunfish","c":"The catch and the cure","d":"Cod quickly dried and lightly salted, sometimes cured in dark rooms for two or three months under salt hay or eelgrass and weights. The name belongs to the small-boat fisheries of eastern Maine and the Bay of Fundy.","w":null},{"t":"Green cod","c":"The catch and the cure","d":"Cod pickled in casks of brine rather than dried; the cure took more salt than kenching.","w":null},{"t":"Tongues","c":"The catch and the cure","d":"Fishermen cut the tongue out of each cod they hauled aboard to keep count of their catch before the fish was salted.","w":null},{"t":"Codfish bounty","c":"The catch and the cure","d":"A federal payment that helped Gloucester fishermen rebuild their fleet after independence.","w":null},{"t":"Surinam trade","c":"The catch and the cure","d":"Gloucester’s trade with Surinam, on the north coast of South America, was under way by 1790 and paid for many of the town’s merchant vessels and wharves until it collapsed in 1863.","w":null},{"t":"Lumper","c":"The catch and the cure","d":"A dock worker who unloads vessels in Gloucester Harbor.","w":null},{"t":"Fish pier","c":"The catch and the cure","d":"The Jodrey State Fish Pier, opened in 1938 and named for Everett R. Jodrey.","w":null},{"t":"Crib wharf","c":"Wharves and the harbor","d":"A wharf built on cribs, rectangular frames of timber that look like a log cabin, filled in, with beams laid over the top crib to carry the deck.","w":null},{"t":"Cob wharf","c":"Wharves and the harbor","d":"A pier of piles and timber filled in with rocks. Gloucester built finger piers and bulkheads this way.","w":null},{"t":"Spiles","c":"Wharves and the harbor","d":"Gloucester’s word for the pilings driven along a wharf as fenders where vessels tie up.","w":null},{"t":"Rafting","c":"Wharves and the harbor","d":"Wharf space in Gloucester Harbor was scarce, so coasting vessels moored together and passed cargo to smaller craft that carried it to nearby towns, and the reverse.","w":null},{"t":"The Stream","c":"Wharves and the harbor","d":"The wide channel at the entrance to the Inner Harbor, where vessels anchored while they waited to unload, often lightering there first.","w":null},{"t":"Marine railway","c":"Wharves and the harbor","d":"A cradle on rails that hauls a vessel out of the water for repair. The Rocky Neck railways, begun in 1859 and worked by a steam engine until about 1970, are said to be the oldest still operating in the country.","w":null},{"t":"The Cut","c":"Wharves and the harbor","d":"The Blynman Canal, dug in 1643 between the harbor and the Annisquam River so that small vessels could avoid the passage around Cape Ann. Storms filled it with sand in 1704 and 1723; it was reopened in 1823.","w":null},{"t":"Dog Bar Breakwater","c":"Wharves and the harbor","d":"The breakwater at Eastern Point that has kept southerly swells out of the harbor since 1905. Stay off it when it is wet or icy.","w":null},{"t":"Five Pound Island","c":"Wharves and the harbor","d":"A small island in the harbor, once fit only for sheep pounds, that later became a flake yard for drying fish.","w":null},{"t":"Essex-built","c":"Wharves and the harbor","d":"Many Gloucester schooners came from the shipyards on the Essex River. The first knockabout was built in Essex in 1902 and the <i>Adventure</i> in 1926; Arthur D. Story launched the <i>Gertrude L. Thebaud</i> there in 1930.","w":null},{"t":"Lay","c":"Life and pay aboard","d":"The formal word for the share system, under which the money from a trip is divided between the boat and the crew.","w":null},{"t":"Settling up","c":"Life and pay aboard","d":"Adding up the trip’s bills and working out each man’s share and the boat’s; the balance sheet is the settlement.","w":null},{"t":"Gross stock and net stock","c":"Life and pay aboard","d":"The gross stock is all the money the boat and crew earned from a trip; the net stock is what is left after the bills charged against it are taken out.","w":null},{"t":"In the hole","c":"Life and pay aboard","d":"When a trip earned less than its expenses, the crew went in the hole and paid the bills from their own pockets. The deficit was the hole bill.","w":null},{"t":"Delegate","c":"Life and pay aboard","d":"The crewman elected to speak for the crew when the settlement is made.","w":null},{"t":"Site","c":"Life and pay aboard","d":"An East Coast word for a berth on a fishing boat, or the promise of one.","w":null},{"t":"Mug-up","c":"Life and pay aboard","d":"A snack between meals: a cup of coffee and a sandwich.","w":null},{"t":"Sou’wester","c":"Life and pay aboard","d":"A wide-brimmed, waterproof oiled hat for foul weather.","w":null},{"t":"Wristlets","c":"Life and pay aboard","d":"Cloth tubes worn on the forearms and held by a thumb loop. They keep the arms warm and wash clean of slime and blood more easily than sleeves.","w":null},{"t":"Ditty bag","c":"Life and pay aboard","d":"A small bag for carrying and stowing a sailor’s personal things.","w":null},{"t":"St. Peter’s Fiesta","c":"Customs, races and memory","d":"Gloucester’s June festival for St. Peter, patron saint of fishermen, first held in 1926 and put on by the Italian-American community: an open-air Mass, processions, seine-boat races, the greasy pole and the blessing of the fleet.","w":null},{"t":"Greasy pole","c":"Customs, races and memory","d":"A heavily greased pole run out over the harbor with a flag at the end, which walkers try to grab before they fall in. It was added to the Fiesta in 1931, after a contest in Terrasini, Sicily, the home town of many Gloucester families.","w":null},{"t":"Viva San Pietro!","c":"Customs, races and memory","d":"“Long live St. Peter!” The cry is shouted in the Fiesta processions and by the greasy-pole walkers.","w":null},{"t":"Blessing of the fleet","c":"Customs, races and memory","d":"On Fiesta Sunday a priest reads out the names of Gloucester’s fishing boats and blesses them. Gloucester’s timeline dates the first blessing to 1945. In 1957 the Portuguese and Italian fleets were blessed on separate Sundays; by 1977 the two communities celebrated together.","w":null},{"t":"Fishermen’s Memorial Service","c":"Customs, races and memory","d":"Each August a procession goes to the Man at the Wheel, where families lay oars and wreaths and cast flowers into the harbor for the men lost at sea.","w":null},{"t":"Cenotaph","c":"Customs, races and memory","d":"The plaques beside the Man at the Wheel bearing the names of Gloucester fishermen lost at sea, more than 5,000 of them. New names are still added.","w":null},{"t":"Man at the Wheel","c":"Customs, races and memory","d":"Leonard Craske’s bronze helmsman on Stacy Boulevard, dedicated in 1925, with the line from Psalm 107, “They that go down to the sea in ships”.","w":null},{"t":"Our Lady of Good Voyage","c":"Customs, races and memory","d":"The church of Gloucester’s Portuguese community, modeled on Santa Maria Madalena in the Azores. Between its towers a statue of Mary holds a two-masted schooner.","w":null},{"t":"Fishermen’s Wives Memorial","c":"Customs, races and memory","d":"The memorial near the Man at the Wheel that honors the women whose faith kept fishing families going.","w":null},{"t":"International Fishermen’s Races","c":"Customs, races and memory","d":"Races between fishing schooners of Gloucester and Nova Scotia, begun in 1920. Lunenburg’s <i>Bluenose</i> never lost a series. The last, sailed off Gloucester and Boston in October 1938 against the <i>Gertrude L. Thebaud</i>, was the final race between North Atlantic sailing fishing schooners.","w":null}],"chains":[{"title":"Lane’s circle","years":"1850s","steps":["William R. G. Mellen","took the Universalist pulpit, 1855","Mary Blood Mellen","worked beside him","Fitz Henry Lane"]},{"title":"Duveneck to Hopper","years":"1890–1923","steps":["Frank Duveneck","brought, 1890s","John H. Twachtman","taught","Leon Kroll","came with, 1912","Edward Hopper","courted here, 1923","Josephine Nivison"]},{"title":"The red cottage","years":"1914–1934","steps":["Charles Allan Winter","urged him to come, 1914","John Sloan","invited, 1915","Stuart Davis"]},{"title":"East Gloucester friends","years":"1919–1926","steps":["Ellen Day Hale","lent a Folly Cove cottage, early 1920s","Theresa Bernstein and William Meyerowitz","hosted","Milton Avery","next-door studio, 1924","Sally Michel"]},{"title":"Avery’s young friends","years":"1932–1945","steps":["Milton Avery","invited, 1932","Mark Rothko, Adolph Gottlieb and Barnett Newman","a studio, 1944; a shared summer, 1945","Aaron Siskind"]}]};
